# core v3.11 — WARN. A consent scope whose value is not a consent scope.
#
# THIS FILE WAS .INVALID.ttl UNTIL 2026-09-02, and the rename is the point of the
# commit that made it. core v3.10 bound cascade:consentScope to a closed list at
# sh:Violation, so this record was REJECTED. core v3.11 opened the list, on
# D-CONSENT-1: "sh:in at sh:Warning at most, never sh:Violation ... a closed list
# missing a member rejects conformant data". Sensitivity categories grow --
# reproductive health and genomics are foreseeable members -- and 42 CFR Part 2
# is US-specific, so a record tagged with a category the list has not caught up
# to is conformant data, not an error.
#
# The fixture's DATA did not change. What changed is what the specification says
# should happen to it, and the polarity suffix is the only place a fixture states
# that. Leaving this file as .INVALID.ttl after v3.11 would have made it assert a
# rejection the specification had deliberately stopped performing, and it fails
# with NO_VIOLATION exactly as it should when that happens. That failure is what
# forced this rename rather than anyone remembering to look.
#
# WHY NOT .VALID.ttl EITHER. .VALID. asserts only the absence of a violation,
# which this record now satisfies -- so it would pass whether or not
# cascade:ConsentScopeShape existed at all, which is the vacuous pass this
# repository exists to catch. .WARN. states the actual post-v3.11 claim: the
# value set still FIRES, at Warning severity, and nothing rejects the record.
#
# THE VALUE IS STILL THE REASON THIS FIXTURE WORKS. cascade:SelfReported is a
# REAL term -- a cascade:DataProvenance subclass declared in core.ttl since v1.
# An invented IRI would be caught by any constraint that merely checked the value
# resolved; an IRI that exists in the same namespace and is simply not a member
# of the cascade:ConsentScope list is caught only by the sh:in constraint. It is
# also the confusion a producer will actually make, provenance being the other
# cascade: code list a record of this shape carries.
#
# WHAT v3.11 DID NOT DEMOTE, and why this fixture cannot show it. The shape
# SPLIT rather than softening: sh:nodeKind, sh:minCount and sh:maxCount keep
# sh:Violation in a block carrying no sh:in, and sh:in moved alone to a second
# block at sh:Warning. So "two consent scopes on one record" and "a literal
# instead of an IRI" are still rejections. Neither has a fixture. Adding them is
# a separate piece of work and they would be .INVALID.ttl, not .WARN.ttl.
#
# EXACTLY ONE FINDING, and no violation:
#   cascade:ConsentScopeShape / path cascade:consentScope / InConstraintComponent
#
# Everything else on this record is valid, which is what keeps the finding
# attributable -- a warn fixture that also violates something is rejected
# outright and is not evidence about the warning.
#
# Fully synthetic.

@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .
@prefix clinical: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/clinical/v1#> .
@prefix health: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/health/v1#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:cascade:social-history:example-consent-scope-wrong-value>
    a clinical:SocialHistoryRecord ;
    clinical:socialHistoryCategory "smokingStatus" ;
    health:smokingStatus "never" ;

    # THE FINDING: a cascade:DataProvenance individual where a
    # cascade:ConsentScope individual belongs. Advisory since core v3.11.
    cascade:consentScope cascade:SelfReported ;

    cascade:dataProvenance cascade:EHRVerified .
